Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 10:36:59 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: NEWS:  Space Mirror

Yesterday's NY TIMES (1/12/93 at p. B5) reports that Russian scientists
plan soon to unfurl a glistening sheet of plastic in space in order to
redirect the sun's light to areas on Earth's dark night side.  The
experiment, called "Znamya" ("Banner"), is scheduled to take place next
month.  The payload will be deployed to unfurl into a 65-foot-diameter disk
of aluminum-coated plastic film.  It will cast the light equivalent of 3 to
5 full moons on an area of Earth measuring about 3 miles in diameter.

"Benefits of extending twilight include savings in the cost of outdoor
lighting, extra hours for planting or harvesting crops, longer working
hours for construction projects and easier rescue operations after
disasters.  Critics worry about possible harm to plants and animals [by
interfering with their circadian rhythms]."

"Russian engineers say it should also teach them a lot about handling thin
sheets of plastic in space, a step toward developing large sails to propel
ships in space using the faint force of sunlight."

The plastic relector being used in the Znamya project has no support frame;
its circular shape will be maintained by rotating its drum.  After 3 to 5
days of tests in an orbit about 225 miles above Earth, the mirror will
re-enter the atmosphere and burn up.

The most ambitious proposals on the table foresee orbiting a constellation
of 100 reflectors, each 1,300 ft in diameter.   Initial operational systems
would post 24 to 36 mirrors, each 650 ft in diameter, in northern
inclination orbits either 620 or 3,700 miles above Earth.  These mirrors
would cast a light equivalent to 50 full moons on Earth areas 35 to 55
miles across.



