Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 11:33:27 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Conflict and Scarcity

> From: salsbury@netcom.com (The Butterfly)

> - Babylon V is the cure for Star Trek blues. It is a universe filled with
> -conflict. Governments large and small, and anarchies. Free traders running
> -about (in fact, one of the main female cast members is a trader).
> -Ray
> 	I'm sorry, I thought this list dealt with the concept of "Dynamic
> Optimism." Is a future where people have gotten over their primate
> tendencies to fight over things too optimistic?

Not merely overoptimistic, but wholely unrealistic.

> Seems that most political
> squabbling boils down to people fighting over limited resources. With a
> nanotech "economy of abundance" this should no longer be the case, no?

No. Nanotech will merely change what gets fought over and at what scale.
Consider -- I've often spoken of a personal desire to have a brain thats
a computer the size of Jupiter. However, there isn't altogether so much free
matter in the solar system that everyone on earth could do that sort of thing.
Scarcity rears its head again.

David Friedman, our local extropian patron saint even if he doesn't want to
be, had a great chapter in "The Machinery of Freedom" on this whole subject,
in which he explained far more clearly than I could why it is that economics
will continue to be a factor in the future.

As an example from the past that Friedman notes, consider this: matches used
to be quite expensive things that people paid for. Nowadays, they get given
away at bars and restaurants for the advertising they can carry. Quite a
change, isn't it? And yet, people now buy and sell automobiles, a commodity
that didn't exist 120 years ago.

> I
> find it fairly enjoyable that they have time to deal with things other than
> "our gun is bigger than his."

Well, I'm afraid its unrealistic. One's desires and reality are often in
conflict. I wish I was able to get any possession I desired just by wanting
it, but unfortunately that doesn't work out -- people have to make things,
and they require an incentive to do so. You might want to live in a universe
without scarcity or war or disputes, but I can present good reasons why
such a universe will never exist -- at the very least, some random mutation
in the gene or meme space will lead some creature to try grabbing more than
his neighbors, and the cycle will restart. "Universal peace with everyone
sharing and living in harmony" just isn't a stable situation -- its ripe
for someone to try to gain massive advantage.

Conflict will always occur -- our hope is prehaps to produce systems that
manage to resolve those conflicts quickly and as peacefully as possible.
Saying "I wish there were no conflicts" is like saying "I wish perpetual
motion was possible".

Perry

