We can compress milk too, but it doesn't tase the same afterwards.  

We may have time for more experiences, but I don't think that they would be
richer... I think that perhaps the oposite would be true.

Compressing an experience requires (I believe) that it be stored somehow...
That works for something like a book, which by nature is a stored medium, 
but how would you store a kiss?  A day in the park? A concert? all of which 
are by nature "Things of the moment"...

I can't help but feel that in storing an experience something would be
lost.  e.g. Going to a concert is more than just listening to the music:
There is the press of bodies around the stage; the adrenaline rush as
the band begins to play; the sound flowing from the speakers and
sweeping you along with it.  But what really makes that experience special 
is its uniqueness, that ephemeral quality.  The best concert I ever attened 
was Sonic Youth at the Warfield, Thurston put his guitar on top of his amp 
and left it there for what seemed like forever.... the sound brought the entire 
hall to its knees.  That is part of my memory... perhaps it could be stored 
somehow and replayed, but then if I could experience that every time I wanted
it, it wouldn't be all that special any more.  

It would no longer be ephemeral.
