Date: Wed, 13 Jan 93 12:19:12 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: CORP Shareholder Interests

> -dj repeated assertion that corporations act only in the interest of
> their shareholders, and most likely 90-day payoff, is totally false.
> Tax laws, especially capital gains taxes and estate taxes, make sure
> that stockholders' interests need not be attended. The main interest
> is that of management and of government, then come special interest
> groups who can pressure the firm hard enough. Shareholders?

Incorrect.

1. A major portion of the shareholders of publicly traded corporations are
TAX EXEMPT retirement investment funds like those invested in by 401K
programs.  These groups change their investments DAILY if stocks fail to
perform.  Long term planning is the least of their worries, since they
are competing with other investment firms for your pre-tax dollars (and
they make a percentage on transactions).  This is why the executive
management team spends virutally all of its time worrying about how to
keep the stock up.

2.  Upper management, the board of directors, and the executive management
teams of corporations are THE LARGEST INDIVIDUAL SHAREHOLDERS!!!  For
instance, Bill Gates is worth $6 billion.  More immediately apparent to
me is that John Sculley has more than 700,000 shares of Apple and untold
options.  Markulla has 2,700,000 shares.  That 12 cent quarterly dividend
means nothing to the average private investor, as he/she doesn't own enough
stock to make much from it. But to Sculley it is $100,000 per quarter!!!
$400,000 per annum!!!  Now, if that dividend were reduced to 11.99 cents per
share, that would result in $1 to $2 million additional funds for R&D
(could fund a long term or risky project), and is invisible to the average
investor.  But to investment funds who have tens or hundreds of millions of
dollars invested, it is very significant.

3.  The board of directors, large shareholders themselves, make the policies
that govern corporations, such as "personnel expenditures can increase 4%
this year", or "layoff 10% of the company".  These are short-term solutions
to keep the Wall Street analysts happy.  Which projects get shit-canned?
Long term, risky, new, or visionary projects.

4.  Ever looked at what percentage of their income high-tech companies spend
on R&D???  At Apple it is just over %8.  This is typical of the industry.
But the board of directors wants to reduce it to %1 or %2, like Dell
or Packard Bell.  Of course clone makers have such low R&D costs because
they are CLONE MAKERS.  They don't invent new hardware, new operating
systems, new paradigms like GUI or desktop video.  The board of directors
and the investors would just as soon see the company be completely statist
and keep making 486-based CPUs forever, since there would be no R&D costs.
In their defence, smaller companies like Genentech spend %50 of their
income on R&D since they are in a brand new industry which hasn't got a
huge prior art or is in a state of incremental changes and small
differentiation.  Yet.

5.  As evidenced by Apple's shareholder report, the executives have an
incredibly incestuous relationship with the corporations.  For instance,
John Sculley leases his private jet to LAS aviation, owned by major
investor Markulla.  Markulla leases the jet to Apple to fly Sculley around
in for over $600,000 per year.  Which is filtered back to Sculley.

I recommend reading "Leaders" by Warren Bennis and "The Virtual Corporation"
by Davidow and Malone.  Davidow was a VP at Intel, and Malone wrote last
year's book about MIPS computer.  These books are a nice introduction into
the literature about corporate America, its failings, and how it must be
changed in order to remain competetive.

Interestingly on the front page of the Business section of the SF Chronicle
this morning is a sidebar about why Tower Records ($600 million per annum)
and Crate & Barrel ($200 million) corporations are NOT public.  Both CEOs
of these companies say that they value quality over quantity, and that
"American investors won't let publicly traded corporations engage in 
long-term planning".

Corporate America is largely risk-averse and focused on short-term gain.
The corporate hierarchical structure, invented to run the railroads in
the 1800s, Taylorian methods, public reporting of earnings every 90 days,
and tax exempt investment funds are all contributing to the downfall of
the American economy, and stand to stifle the development and commercialization
of long range technologies.

-dj

