>> There are likely numerous
>> genes for intellectualism, understanding of complex mathematics and large
>> systems, dynamism, etc.

>I find it highly unlikely that there is any such thing.  You might as well
>expect there to exist "the neuron for remembering your grandmother's face".
>Just because humans exhibit certain traits in varying degrees, traits whose
>possession is to some extent dependent on ancestry, does not imply that
>there are genes for those traits or that it is possible to strongly select
>for or against them. 

You are making an arbitrary distinction between mental characteristics and
"pysical" characteristics.  Physical characteristics are selected and
determined by genes.  Mental characteristics are also, to some extent,
determined by physical makeup (your brain and nervous system).  Naturally
there will be genes to determine some set of mental characteristics.
The posit was that some things like logic or visualization ability or
complex mathematics or some larger set of abilities _could_ be tied
to a physical gene.  In adult training programs, it has been found that
up to 60% of people cannot be trained to understand "problem synthesis".
Is this purely enivronmental, or are people's brains different, in just
the same way that their skin color, physical build, eye color, etc are
different.

-dj


